// Horror: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== horror.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Tenant Before
genre: horror + psychological-horror
tone: Domestic, patient and wrong by degrees
era: - | contemporary
setting: A terraced house in Leeds | real | home
logline: A night-shift nurse renting her first house alone finds that the previous tenant's post, habits and handwriting keep arriving, and that the landlord has no record of anyone living there before her.
premise: {tamsin} moves into 14 Carr Lane after a divorce, glad of a place nobody else has a key to. Letters come for a Mrs. H. Arkwright. The heating runs to a schedule {tamsin} never set. A shopping list appears on the fridge pad in a hand that is not hers, for things she then finds she has bought. Her neighbour {viv} remembers the woman before. The landlord, {curtis}, says the house stood empty for nine years.
synopsis: {tamsin} works nights on a stroke ward and sleeps through the days in a house she is proud of. The first signs are administrative: post for Mrs. H. Arkwright, a milk order she didn't place, a thermostat with opinions. She returns the letters and resets the timer. Then the list. Then she wakes at four in the afternoon in a made bed she does not remember making, with her hair pinned in a style she can't do. {viv} next door, kind and avid, describes Hetty Arkwright exactly: a widow who nursed her husband in the back bedroom and kept the house "going" long after. {curtis} shows {tamsin} the tenancy file: nobody since 2015. {tamsin}, who charts other people's lost hours for a living, starts charting her own. On the ward her patient {enid}, aphasic since her stroke, begins saying one clear word when {tamsin} is near: "Hetty." The second act is {tamsin} trying to prove she is being haunted rather than ill, because a haunting can be left and an illness goes with you. She films herself asleep. The footage shows her rise at noon, dress, and keep house for six hours with a competence and tenderness she has never had, talking to an empty chair in the back bedroom. It is not frightening to watch; that is what frightens her. She learns that the house was not empty: Hetty died in it, and {curtis}'s mother, who owned it, simply never told the file. In the third act {tamsin} stops fighting the hours and reads what Hetty writes in them. The lists become letters. Hetty is not trying to take her; she is trying to finish a shift, and cannot hand over to someone who will not take a handover. {tamsin} sits in the back bedroom at noon, awake, and does what she does every night on the ward: she takes the report, says the patient's name, and says "I have him now." The heating clicks off. She keeps the house. She leaves the chair.
question: Is {tamsin} losing herself, or being trusted with something?
themes: care as an inheritance; the work nobody sees outliving the worker; solitude against loneliness
audience: Adults who like horror that unsettles more than it startles
viewpoint: With {tamsin}, including what she can only know from footage of herself
stakes: Her sanity as she understands it, her nursing registration if she is found unfit, and the first home she has had alone
conflict: {tamsin}'s need to be the sole occupant of her own life against a house that has someone else's routine in its walls
ending: {tamsin} takes the handover. The house goes quiet and stays hers. Months on, she finds a list on the fridge in her own handwriting, and one item at the bottom in the other hand: "Thank you."
experience: Creeping recognition rather than shock: the dread of tidiness, then an ache where the scare should be
look: Daylight horror. Net curtains, clean surfaces, the same four rooms at different hours; the camera never moves unless she does
sound: A boiler, a carriage clock, pips on a ward monitor that the house seems to keep time with. No music until the handover.
m: understand | past | belonging-vs-identity | reconciled
rule: The house keeps Hetty's hours, noon to six | Nothing happens at night, when {tamsin} is at work and awake
rule: What is done in those hours is done by {tamsin}'s hands | Nothing moves on its own; there is nothing to film but her
@ tamsin | Tamsin Ogunleye-Marsh | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 41 | A house that is only hers | She spent fifteen years being half of something | Six hours a day she cannot account for | Meticulous about others' charts and vague about her own needs | From guarding her solitude to accepting company she didn't choose | Brisk, clinical, dry jokes at her own expense | Scrubs under a parka, lanyard marks on her neck
@ viv | Viv Castleton | supporting | ally | human | she/her | 68 | To be useful and to be told things | The street has emptied of people she knew | She likes the story too much to be careful with it | Generous, nosy, and right | From spectator to witness | Chatty, parenthetical, lowers her voice for names | Quilted gilet, binoculars "for the birds"
@ curtis | Curtis Hallam | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 37 | The rent on time and no surprises | He inherited eleven houses and a filing cabinet he has never opened | He believes his paperwork | Decent, harassed, incurious | From "nobody lived there" to bringing the missing file himself | Estate-agent cheerful; trails off | Fleece with a logo, van keys always in hand
@ enid | Enid Brathwaite | featured | none | human | she/her | 83 | To make one thing understood | A stroke has taken every word but one | Nobody on the ward knows what the word refers to | Imperious even without speech | Unchanged; she is the bell that rings | One word, clearly, and a pointing hand | Bed jacket, hair set weekly by a daughter we never see
@ dorothy | Sister Dorothy Achebe | featured | mentor | human | she/her | 55 | A safe ward and a nurse who sleeps | She has seen good staff break quietly | {tamsin} is hiding it well | Stern on the floor, soft in the sluice room | From watching to asking | Economical; ends sentences with a look | Navy uniform, reading glasses on her head
~ tamsin - viv | new neighbour and the street's memory | {tamsin} avoids her | {tamsin} brings her tea and asks for all of it
~ tamsin - curtis | tenant and landlord | reassurance and a shrug | he hands her a death certificate and an apology
~ tamsin - enid | nurse and patient | routine care | the one person who says the name
~ tamsin - dorothy | staff nurse and ward sister | concealment | {tamsin} asks for two weeks of days
# Arrival | INT | 14 Carr Lane, Hallway | DAY | tamsin, curtis | Give her the house | Keys, an inventory, a boiler he calls temperamental. Post already on the mat for Mrs. H. Arkwright | | 3 | props=keys+inventory+letters
# Arrival | INT | Stroke Ward, Bay Two | NIGHT | tamsin, enid, dorothy | Show what she is good at | A night round; {tamsin} charts {enid}'s hours exactly and hands over at seven with the phrase she will need later | | 4 | props=obs chart+handover sheet
# Arrival | INT | 14 Carr Lane, Kitchen | DAY | tamsin | The list | She wakes at four. On the fridge pad: bread, Vim, his tablets, in copperplate. In the cupboard, all three | {tamsin} finds she has done the shopping | 4 | props=fridge notepad+groceries
# Evidence | EXT | Carr Lane, Front Gardens | DAY | tamsin, viv | Learn who came before | {viv}, over the wall, describes Hetty down to the hairpins and the back bedroom | {tamsin} touches her own pinned hair | 4
# Evidence | INT | Landlord's Office | DAY | tamsin, curtis | Be told she is wrong | {curtis} shows her the file: empty since 2015. He is kind about it | {tamsin} would rather be haunted than ill | 3 | props=tenancy file
# Evidence | INT | 14 Carr Lane, Front Room | NIGHT | tamsin | Watch herself | The footage: she rises at noon, dresses, cleans, cooks for two, and sits talking in the back bedroom. She is gentle. She is good at it | It is her, and it is not frightening | 6 | props=laptop+camera on tripod
# Evidence | INT | Stroke Ward, Bay Two | NIGHT | tamsin, enid, dorothy | The word | {enid} takes {tamsin}'s wrist and says "Hetty," twice, pointing at her hands. {dorothy} sees {tamsin}'s face | {dorothy} asks | 4
# Handover | INT | 14 Carr Lane, Hallway | DAY | tamsin, curtis, viv | Fill in the file | {curtis} brings his mother's box: a death certificate, 2015, this address. {viv} stands in the doorway and says nothing for once | The house was never empty | 4 | props=box file+death certificate
# Handover | INT | 14 Carr Lane, Back Bedroom | DAY | tamsin | Take the report | Noon. She stays awake, sits by the empty bed, reads the day's list aloud as a handover, says his name, and says "I have him now" | The heating clicks off | 6 | props=handover sheet+carriage clock
# Handover | INT | 14 Carr Lane, Kitchen | DAY | tamsin | Keep the house | Months on. Her own list on the fridge, and one line under it in copperplate | | 2 | props=fridge notepad
